    "explanation": "To both parties. You were billed SGD 39.40 (fare breakdown EV-FARE-02810) for a trip the GPS trace EV-GPS-02810 records at 31.2 km against a booked optimal of 24.0 km (trip record EV-TRIP-02810). Under POL-RD-01 the trace figure governs the distance, and it shows an excess of 7.2 km, 30.0% of optimal. Both establishment tests pass (above 4.8 km and above 0.8 km), so a deviation is established. The deviation is not MATERIAL (30% is under 50% and 7.2 km is under 8.0 km), so the POL-RD-06 driver-profile check is not engaged; in any event driver_profile.deviation_flags_90d is 0 against 196 completed trips in 90 days, so there is no repeat-pattern uplift and no conduct referral. The lifetime counter prior_deviation_flags was mentioned only to say it is not the field POL-RD-06 names; it is context-only under POL-GEN-01 and was not used as a ground.\n\nThe driver's message 'AYE closed, I had to divert' cites no evidence ID and carries no weight by itself under POL-RD-08. The record does, however, hold the LTA advisory EV-ADVIS-02810-01 (weight 1.0): it names the AYE towards Tuas after Clementi Ave 6, a segment the booked optimal route traverses, and was in force 20:39:44–22:15:24, overlapping the trip window (21:09:44–21:50:24) by 70.7 minutes. The trace itself is unimpaired (245 fixes, 10 s interval, 100% coverage, zero gaps, max gap 0 s), so POL-RD-07 does not engage and no independent corroboration is needed. On the actual route the trace stays entirely clear of the closed stretch (0 fixes inside the advisory geometry, minimum approach 1,699.5 m); because the optimal route runs through that stretch, a route that avoids it necessarily left the optimal corridor around the affected segment and rejoined it, so POL-RD-03(c) is met. I therefore did not accept the argument, made on both sides, that POL-RD-03 fails outright because the trace never enters the advisory's area: avoiding the closed area is what a diversion looks like, and the derived advisory-area figure was one to weigh, not one that decides by itself.\n\nExoneration under POL-RD-03 is capped: min(7.2, 1.30 x (26.0 - 24.0) + 1.00) = 3.60 km. The closure explains 3.60 km of the 7.2 km excess and no more; the remaining 3.6 km is refundable. The closure-adjusted optimal is 24.0 + 3.60 = 27.60 km, with duration 32.0 x (27.60 / 24.0) = 36.8 min.\n\nThe refund is arithmetic under POL-RD-02. The fare model is METERED and the surge multiplier of 1.0 is read from fare_breakdown.surge_multiplier (EV-FARE-02810). recomputed_fare = (3.80 + 0.75 x 27.60 + 0.28 x 36.8) x 1.0 + 0.80 platform fee + 0 ERP = 35.604. charged_total = 39.40. refund = 39.40 - 35.604 = 3.796, rounded up to SGD 3.80. That is above the SGD 0.50 de minimis and 9.6% of the charged total, well below the 90% full-refund share, so the outcome is a partial refund of SGD 3.80.\n\nOther points raised are answered as follows. No rider-requested detour is available: the chat log EV-CHAT-02810 holds zero messages, so POL-RD-04 cannot be run and no distance is excluded on that ground. No pick-up or drop-off amendment exists (both timestamps null and the amended optimal distance null), so POL-RD-05 cannot be run and the booked 24.0 km baseline stands. The vehicle is RYDE_X, not RYDE_POOL, so POL-RD-12's matched-itinerary baseline does not apply. POL-RD-09 adds nothing: the fare breakdown carries no ERP lines and no airport surcharge, so there is no pass-through correction and no billing-integrity finding. The rider's profile does not trigger the POL-RD-08 corroboration overlay (deviation_claims_60d is 1, below 4). No POL-RD-10 escalation trigger fires: the trip identifiers match across trip record, trace and fare breakdown, the trace is unimpaired, the timestamps agree, the refund is far below SGD 150.00 and below the charged total, and every charge line has a counterpart in the record.\n\nSGD 3.80 is below the SGD 8.00 automatic-execution threshold, so it will be executed on issue. Each party may appeal once within 30 days under POL-GEN-04.",
